 ## Overview
-Entropy encoding is a lossless compression method that minimizes storage or transmission costs for a discrete dataset by mapping each data point to a variable-length bit string, which a receiver inverts to recover the exact original data [S2]. It solves the problem of data redundancy by assigning shorter codewords to more frequent symbols and longer codewords to less frequent symbols [S3, S4, S5]. Unlike its parent [Arithmetic Encoding](2127-arithmetic-encoding.md), which encodes symbol sequences into a single real-valued interval, entropy encoding generally refers to the broader class of statistical compression including Huffman coding and arithmetic coding, where the core distinction lies in how the bitstream is constructed from symbol probabilities [S3]. It differs from [Variable-Length Coding](7012-variable-length-coding.md) in that VLC assigns a fixed bit depth to an entire data block based on local range, whereas entropy encoding assigns codes based on individual symbol frequency [S1].
+Entropy encoding is a lossless compression method that minimizes storage or transmission costs for a discrete dataset by mapping each data point to a variable-length bit string, which a receiver inverts to recover the exact original data [S2]. It solves the problem of data redundancy by assigning shorter codewords to more frequent symbols and longer codewords to less frequent symbols [S3, S4, S5]. It encompasses methods like Huffman coding and arithmetic coding, which differ in how they map symbol probabilities to bit sequences [S3][S6, unofficial]. It differs from [Variable-Length Coding](7012-variable-length-coding.md), which assigns a fixed bit depth to an entire data block based on its local value range, by assigning codes based on individual symbol frequency [S1, S3].
 
 ## How it works
-Entropy encoding operates by analyzing the statistical distribution of input symbols and mapping them to binary codewords. The two primary implementations are Huffman Coding and Arithmetic Coding.
+Entropy encoding operates by analyzing the statistical distribution of input symbols and mapping them to binary codewords [S3]. The two primary implementations are Huffman Coding and Arithmetic Coding [S3][S6, unofficial].
 
